# Project: GameTracker
A video game library and tracking app, conceptually similar to Letterboxd but for games.

## Stack
- React + Vite (web app, runs at localhost:5173 in dev)
- Capacitor wrapped for iOS (capacitor.config.json + ios/ folder exist) — this is also a native iOS app, not just web
- Game data sourced from IGDB API via a backend proxy in src/services/igdb.js (IGDB does not allow direct browser calls due to CORS; all IGDB requests go through our proxy with cached Twitch OAuth tokens)

## File structure conventions
- Pages live in src/pages/
- Reusable components in src/components/
- Custom hooks in src/hooks/
- API services and data layer in src/services/
- Shared styles in src/styles/, plus App.css and index.css at src/ root
- Types (if used) in src/types/
- App entry: src/main.jsx → src/App.jsx

## Design system
- Dark theme: deep navy background, warm copper/amber accent
- Typography: serif display headers ("Currently Playing", "Your Library"), sans-serif body
- Five bottom tabs: Home, Explore, Search, Library, Profile
- Reference DESIGN_SYSTEM.md at the project root for color tokens and any other design-system documentation

## Domain rules
- Game tracker statuses: Want to Play / Playing / Played / Dropped
- A game has at most one status at a time (mutually exclusive); do not implement as separate booleans
- All status changes must be optimistic — update UI immediately, then sync to store

## Conventions
- Match existing file structure; do not create new top-level folders without reason
- Use existing utility functions and components before creating new ones
- Plain JavaScript (.js / .jsx), not TypeScript — match existing code
- Use existing color tokens / CSS variables; do not hardcode hex colors
- Capacitor-aware: when adding interactive features (haptics, share, native menus), check if a Capacitor plugin exists before falling back to web-only APIs
- All animations must respect `prefers-reduced-motion`

## Reference docs in this repo
The repo has many .md files documenting prior work (DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, FEATURES_OVERVIEW.md, NAVIGATION_UPDATE.md, MOBILE_GRID_FIX.md, etc.). When making changes that overlap a documented area, read the relevant .md first to avoid contradicting prior decisions.